{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is the ChatGPT architecture?

ChatGPT is a conversational AI product: a user sends a message, the service assembles
the allowed conversation context, selects a model and tools, generates a response,
applies safety policy, and streams the result back. The language model is one expensive
dependency inside a larger distributed system that also owns admission, routing,
memory, tool authorization, feedback, storage, and operations.

In plain language, the architecture turns token generation into a responsive and
controlled product. The core invariant is: **an admitted request follows the required
safety and authorization path, and every degradation is explicit to the user and to
operators.** A faster answer is not correct if it bypasses policy, repeats a tool side
effect, leaks another conversation, or silently uses stale state.

This case study presents a measured reference design based on observable product
requirements and general LLM-serving principles. It does not claim to describe
OpenAI's private deployment. Review [Model Serving](/ml-systems/model-serving) first
if batching, accelerators, and online inference are unfamiliar.
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the conversation contract and scope" %}

## Define what one chat turn must promise

A chat turn begins when the service accepts a user message and ends with a completed,
cancelled, refused, or failed response. That lifecycle needs explicit states because a
stream can disconnect after some tokens arrive and a tool can finish after its caller
times out.

### Functional scope

- Accept a text message for an authenticated conversation and assign a unique request ID.
- Build a bounded model input from instructions, recent turns, a conversation summary,
  and authorized tool results.
- Select an eligible model tier and healthy serving region.
- Stream response tokens, support cancellation, and record a clear finish reason.
- Invoke allowlisted tools through typed arguments and return either a typed result or
  typed failure to the model.
- Preserve conversation history according to product retention and deletion rules.

### Design targets for this exercise

- **Responsiveness:** keep p95 time to first token below two seconds for admitted,
  non-tool requests under the planned load.
- **Availability:** continue safe text generation through a single worker or regional
  serving failure when reserve capacity exists.
- **Isolation:** never mix one user's context, cache entries, tool credentials, or
  streamed output with another request.
- **Side effects:** execute a tool action at most once for one idempotency key, even if
  the model or client retries.
- **Safety:** never turn a required policy dependency failure into an unfiltered model
  request.

### Deliberate exclusions

- Model training and dataset construction are outside the online serving path.
- Voice, image, and video pipelines are omitted so token serving remains the focus.
- Exact model internals, fleet sizes, vendors, prices, and private deployment details
  are not assumed.
- Perfect factual accuracy is not guaranteed by generation; tools and retrieval need
  their own freshness and provenance contracts.

{% callout variant="info" %}
Time to first token and tokens per second measure different parts of the experience.
Admission, policy, routing, queueing, and prompt processing happen before the first
token. Decode throughput determines how quickly the rest of the response arrives.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="capacity" title="2. Estimate tokens, throughput, cost, and model mix" %}

## Convert conversations into serving work

Requests per second are not enough to size an LLM service. Prompt length controls
prefill work and cache pressure, generated length controls serial decode work, and the
model tier changes both cost and worker throughput.

For an illustrative baseline of 120 requests/s, 1,200 input tokens per request, and
320 generated tokens per response:

- Input rate is `120 x 1,200 = 144,000 tokens/s`.
- Output rate is `120 x 320 = 38,400 tokens/s`.
- Raw token rate is `182,400 tokens/s`, or about 15.8 billion tokens/day at a constant
  load.
- Actual serving capacity still depends on model size, hardware, batching, cache reuse,
  sequence mix, and the reserved utilization target.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="120 req/s" label="Illustrative load" detail="Admitted chat turns at the baseline." tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="144K/s" label="Input tokens" detail="Prompt and context processed before decode." tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="38.4K/s" label="Output tokens" detail="Generated serially and streamed to users." tone="green" /%}
{% metric value="28% reserve" label="Planning headroom" detail="The lab targets 72% steady utilization." tone="amber" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Test the serving envelope

Use the lab to separate five decisions that are often collapsed into one estimate.
Increase context length without changing traffic, switch model tiers at the same load,
then raise output length until the queue grows. The cost values and per-worker rates
are illustrative assumptions so the arithmetic is inspectable rather than a claim
about a production fleet.

{% interactive-block id="case-studies/openai-chatgpt-serving-capacity-lab" dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/openai-chatgpt/data/serving-capacity-model.json" /%}

### Interpret the result

1. **Raw tokens describe product demand.** They make input and generated volume visible.
2. **Serving units describe the planning model.** Input tokens receive a tier-specific
   weight while generated tokens retain full weight.
3. **Headroom is intentional capacity.** It absorbs bursts, retries, uneven sequence
   lengths, and worker loss; it is not wasted hardware.
4. **Tier routing is a product policy.** A cheaper tier is useful only when it still
   satisfies the task's quality and safety requirements.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Separate admission, orchestration, and inference" %}

## Keep the public boundary away from model workers

The gateway owns whether work enters the system. The orchestrator owns what context,
model, region, and tools the request may use. The model pool owns prompt processing
and token generation. Separating those responsibilities prevents a queue spike or
worker failure from removing authentication, quotas, or safety controls.

{% system-flow title="One admitted generation request" caption="The response can stream incrementally, but every stage remains observable under the same request ID." %}
{% system-node title="Gateway and admission" eyebrow="Protect" icon="shield" tone="blue" %}
Authenticate, enforce request and token limits, assign an idempotency key, and reject
work that cannot fit a bounded queue.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Policy and context" eyebrow="Assemble" icon="database" tone="rose" %}
Evaluate input risk and build a size-bounded prompt from instructions, conversation
state, and authorized data.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Router and model pool" eyebrow="Place" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Choose an eligible tier and region, batch compatible work, run prefill, and decode
tokens incrementally.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Filter, stream, and record" eyebrow="Deliver" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Apply output controls, stream tokens, persist the finish state, and emit privacy-bounded
service, safety, quality, and cost signals.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Request lifecycle

1. The gateway authenticates the caller, validates payload size, assigns a request ID,
   and reserves queue capacity.
2. Input policy returns an allow, restrict, or refuse decision with a policy version.
3. The orchestrator loads only the permitted conversation state and trims it to the
   selected tier's context budget.
4. The router selects a compatible model revision, region, and worker pool from health,
   queue depth, data-residency, and product policy.
5. The scheduler batches compatible prefill or decode work without allowing a long
   sequence to hold every interactive request behind it.
6. Output policy evaluates the stream, the client receives ordered chunks, and the
   completion record captures a final reason such as stop, length, cancellation,
   refusal, or failure.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Streaming improves perceived latency, not total compute. The system still pays for
every processed input token and every generated output token until completion or
cancellation reaches the worker.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="state-and-tools" title="4. Manage model serving, memory, and tools" %}

## Treat generated context as data, not hidden memory

The model does not automatically remember a conversation between requests. The product
must reconstruct each turn from stored state. That state has different trust levels,
retention rules, and failure behavior.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Recent turns" eyebrow="Verbatim context" icon="repeat" tone="blue" %}
The newest user and assistant messages preserve local coherence. They consume context
quickly and may contain untrusted instructions, so the service applies a token budget
and clear role boundaries.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Conversation summary" eyebrow="Compressed context" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
A versioned summary carries older facts at lower token cost. It can be incomplete or
wrong, so it remains model context rather than an authoritative user profile.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Authoritative state" eyebrow="Tools and product data" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Account settings, permissions, orders, and other mutable facts stay in systems of
record. A tool reads them under user authorization and returns provenance with the
result.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Reuse attention state without crossing isolation boundaries

During prefill, the model processes the prompt and creates attention key/value state.
During decode, it reuses that state while generating one token at a time. A KV cache
trades accelerator memory for less repeated computation, but its key must include the
conversation, model revision, and exact tokenized prefix.

{% code-block
   file="/api/content/case-studies/openai-chatgpt/code/kv-cache-sketch.py"
   language="python"
   title="Conversation-scoped KV-cache reuse"
/%}

### Batch only compatible work

Dynamic batching improves throughput by placing multiple requests in one model step.
The scheduler still needs memory checks, tier and revision compatibility, deadlines,
fairness, and cancellation. Maximizing batch size without those constraints can make
interactive latency worse.

{% code-block
   file="/api/content/case-studies/openai-chatgpt/code/dynamic-batching-sketch.py"
   language="python"
   title="Deadline-aware batch formation"
/%}

### Make a tool call a controlled state machine

1. The model proposes a tool name and structured arguments; it does not receive raw
   service credentials.
2. The broker validates the schema, checks the user's authorization, and rejects
   disallowed arguments before execution.
3. The tool runs with a deadline, idempotency key, audit context, and a narrow response
   schema.
4. The broker returns a typed success or failure. The model may retry only when policy
   marks the operation safe and the retry budget remains.
5. The final response distinguishes tool-backed facts from model-generated explanation.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="safety-feedback" title="5. Layer safety controls and feedback" %}

## Keep policy decisions on both sides of generation

Safety is not one classifier and it is not only model training. The online system uses
multiple controls because each catches a different failure and has a different latency
budget.

- **Before generation:** enforce authentication, age or product restrictions, abuse
  rate limits, input policy, tool eligibility, and context isolation.
- **During generation:** bound tokens and time, validate tool calls, detect cancellation,
  and apply streaming policy where the product requires it.
- **After generation:** store the finish reason and policy versions, accept reports,
  sample privacy-bounded traces, and route uncertain incidents to trained reviewers.
- **Outside the request path:** run red-team suites, offline evaluations, abuse analysis,
  and staged model or policy rollouts before increasing exposure.

### Feedback is evidence, not an immediate model update

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Capture" label="Bounded evidence" %}
Record explicit ratings, reports, completion state, route, latency, and policy versions
under the applicable consent, retention, and access rules.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Triage" label="Separate owners" %}
Send service failures to reliability owners, policy incidents to safety review, and
quality regressions to evaluation queues instead of mixing them into one score.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Evaluate" label="Reproducible gates" %}
Build reviewed examples and compare candidate model, prompt, tool, and policy versions
against quality, safety, latency, and cost guardrails.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Release" label="Controlled exposure" %}
Canary the compatible change, monitor segmented outcomes, retain rollback artifacts,
and expand only while all required guardrails hold.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not use raw conversation logs as unrestricted analytics data. Minimize collection,
separate access roles, honor deletion and retention policy, and prefer derived metrics
when operators do not need the message text.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="failures" title="6. Route around failures without bypassing policy" %}

## Choose degraded behavior before an incident

A resilient chat service does not promise that every request always reaches the largest
model. It promises bounded admission, preserved safety and authorization, explicit
fallbacks, and an observable final state.

Use the topology lab to compare a healthy text turn with regional capacity loss, a
required safety-dependency failure, and a tool timeout. The active route, failed or
degraded node, selected component, and user-visible result change together.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/case-studies/openai-chatgpt/data/serving-failure-topology.json" /%}

### Failure rules the diagram makes visible

- **Queue overload:** reject or defer bounded low-priority work at admission. An
  unbounded queue converts overload into a long outage and wastes work users may cancel.
- **Worker loss:** retry only before visible output or resume under an explicit product
  contract. A second stream must not silently duplicate the first response.
- **Region loss:** preserve input policy, authorization, and request identity while
  routing eligible traffic to reserved compatible capacity.
- **Safety dependency loss:** use a restricted deterministic path for affected work;
  never reinterpret missing policy as permission.
- **Tool timeout:** return one typed failure, stop retry amplification, and use the same
  idempotency key if a side effect may have reached the provider.
- **Conversation-store degradation:** continue only with context already attached to
  the request, label the limited state, and avoid overwriting a newer summary during
  recovery.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="7. Operate latency, quality, safety, and cost together" %}

## Measure each boundary of the product contract

One global success rate cannot explain a slow or unsafe conversation. Telemetry should
join on request ID and be segmented by model tier, model revision, region, queue,
context-length band, cache state, tool, policy version, client version, and fallback
reason.

### Service and capacity signals

- Admission rate, rejection reason, queue depth and age, cancellations, and retries.
- Time to first token split into gateway, policy, context load, queue, and prefill time.
- Decode tokens/s, stream completion rate, output length, worker utilization, batch
  occupancy, KV-cache bytes, and cache evictions.
- Input and output tokens per request, estimated cost per successful turn, and capacity
  by model tier and region.

### Product, tool, and safety signals

- Finish reason, user-visible errors, conversation continuation, explicit ratings, and
  quality evaluation slices.
- Tool proposal, authorization result, timeout, typed error, side-effect idempotency,
  and provenance presence.
- Policy decision and version, restricted-path rate, reports, appeal outcomes, and
  reviewed false-positive and false-negative samples.
- Privacy access logs, retention enforcement, deletion completion, and unauthorized
  context-access attempts.

### Incident and release loop

1. Detect the breached boundary and freeze unrelated rollout changes that would blur
   diagnosis.
2. Contain impact with admission limits, output caps, tier routing, tool circuit
   breakers, or regional evacuation while safety remains enforced.
3. Verify recovery with user-visible SLIs, not only worker health; then drain queues in
   age order and prevent cancelled work from reappearing.
4. Reconstruct representative request timelines from versioned, privacy-bounded
   evidence and update capacity assumptions, tests, and runbooks.
5. Roll out model, prompt, policy, or scheduler changes to a bounded cohort with a
   compatible rollback version and predeclared stop conditions.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="trade-offs" title="8. Make the serving trade-offs explicit" %}

## Optimize the whole conversation, not one metric

The architecture is a set of coupled product decisions. Improving one measure can move
cost, latency, safety, privacy, or quality elsewhere.

- **Larger model versus routed tiers:** one large tier simplifies behavior but spends
  scarce capacity on simple turns. Routing lowers cost and queue pressure but needs
  task classification, eligibility rules, evaluation by route, and clear fallback
  semantics.
- **Large batches versus interactive latency:** larger batches improve accelerator
  utilization. Short batching windows and fairness controls protect time to first token
  for live conversations.
- **Long context versus selective memory:** more history can preserve detail but raises
  prefill time, cost, privacy exposure, and distraction. Summaries save tokens but must
  be versioned and treated as fallible context.
- **Regional duplication versus simpler operations:** reserve capacity improves failover
  and latency but increases cost, model-distribution work, data-governance constraints,
  and compatibility testing.
- **Aggressive tool autonomy versus controlled execution:** more tools can complete more
  tasks, but every side effect adds authorization, idempotency, timeout, audit, and user
  confirmation requirements.

### A defensible first design

1. Start with bounded text chat, explicit finish states, one general model tier, no
   side-effecting tools, and a strict context budget.
2. Add a faster tier only after route-specific quality and safety evaluations show which
   requests may move without violating the product contract.
3. Add tools behind a typed broker with user authorization, idempotency, deadlines, and
   provenance before expanding the allowlist.
4. Reserve regional recovery capacity and rehearse failover while measuring admission,
   time to first token, stream completion, policy decisions, and cost together.

### What to carry into another design

- Tokens are both the workload unit and a major cost driver, but model tier and sequence
  shape determine how those tokens consume capacity.
- Conversation history is reconstructed state, not durable memory inside the model.
- Safety, authorization, and idempotency are serving-path invariants, not optional
  post-processing.
- Streaming, batching, caching, and model routing improve different bottlenecks and need
  separate metrics.
- Feedback becomes useful only after privacy controls, review, reproducible evaluation,
  and a controlled release path.

Continue with [LLM Serving and APIs](/genai/llm-serving) for deeper inference mechanics
and [GenAI Monitoring](/genai/genai-monitoring) for evaluation and production telemetry.

